    findings=[('Regulate price authority jointly with service','At the central calibration, commercial joint control lowers railway-centered welfare in all three corridors; fare-cap and elasticity tests delimit this result.'),('Treat polarization as a composition effect','Equal-mean polarization lowers the incremental commercial value of seat pricing in all three cases; moderate shocks are mostly absorbed.'),('Distinguish commercial and welfare K entry','Commercial K enables profitable G/D co-adjustment but can reduce traveler surplus. JX welfare entry depends strongly on schedule value and availability.'),('Budget explicitly for a mobility guarantee','Strict retention raises welfare while reducing operator profit; per-trip operator costs are 17--52 times reference L fares.'),('Keep a participation safeguard','A lower realized-traveler cost ratio can coexist with lost vulnerable trips; the divergence affects a material part of the tested design library.'),('Interpret supply flexibility within resources','Protected G/D quotas permit period changes, but reference-sized fleets restrict aggregate expansion; looser fleets mainly improve JX welfare in the tested reference states.')]
